From d5bbe23cc0b7b70fccc4d5e90bba30499784c03d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Van Tran <tetcu@fgorg.vn>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:09:03 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(workflow): owner gates, workflows by Type and their
 board columns, approval panel, send-back limit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- feat(workflow): agents cannot move an issue out of a step a person handles

  A step whose handler resolves to a member is an owner gate. An agent actor
  (task token) changing the status or the project of an issue in such a step
  gets 403 {code: owner_gate}; the batch endpoint skips the issue. Members are
  unaffected; agents still move issues into the gate.

- feat(issues): approval panel for the person a workflow step waits on

  While an issue sits in a step whose handler is the viewer (a member, or the
  creator when that is the viewer), the issue page shows an approval panel:
  Approve moves it to the step's next status, Send back asks what must change,
  posts it as a comment and moves it to the step's back status.

- feat(workflow): pick a project's workflow by a select property (Type)

  Table project_workflow_rule (add-only, no foreign keys): in a project, an
  issue whose select property has an option follows another workflow; anything
  else follows the project's workflow. ForIssue resolves rule -> project ->
  Default for issue writes, create, the claim brief and the gate check.
  API GET/PUT /api/projects/{id}/workflow-rules (owner/admin). Changing the
  property moves a status the new workflow lacks to its equivalent, like a
  project move; an agent cannot change it while the issue waits on a person.
  Workspace deletion removes the rules.

- feat(projects): edit workflows by Type in the project sidebar

  A "By Type" row under the project's workflow: summary of the rules, and for
  owners/admins a popover to pick the select property and, per option, the
  workflow its issues follow (or the project's workflow).

- feat(boards): a project board shows the columns of every workflow its rules use

  When a project sends some Types to other workflows (workflow-by-type), its
  board columns are the project workflow's steps plus each rule workflow's
  middle steps, before blocked/done/cancelled, so every issue has a column.
  Without rules nothing changes.

- feat(issues): pull requests in the approval panel

  While an issue waits for the viewer's approval, the panel lists its pull requests
  — the PRs linked to the issue, then GitHub PR links in its description and
  comments, newest first, up to three — each with "Approve on GitHub" (opens the
  PR's review page) and a note to approve the PR there, then Approve here.
  Reference: Jira's development panel on approvals.

- feat(issues): the property a project's workflow depends on is always shown

  In a project whose workflow rules read a property (Type), the create-issue form
  and the issue page show that property without "Add property", so the work type
  is picked first, as Jira puts the work type first. Core: ruleProperties.

- feat(properties): a select property derived from another (Domain from Type)

  A select property whose description says "Derived from <Source>" takes the
  option named like the part of the source option before " · " (Type "Tech ·
  Feature" → Domain "Tech"), when an issue is created and whenever the source
  changes. Left alone when no option matches. One property to pick, the other
  follows.

- feat(workflow): cap how often agents send an issue back

  A workflow may set max_send_backs (GET/PUT /api/issue-workflows/{id}/limits,
  table vantt_workflow_limit). An agent moving an issue along a step's back
  edge after that many agent send-backs with no person's status move in
  between gets 409 {code: send_back_limit}; the batch endpoint skips it. A
  person's move is never refused and starts a fresh count. No limit set =
  upstream behaviour.

Patch-Id: workflow
